# Mathematics still to be completed
#
# Section IV — Theorem IV.2
# Existence of a finite prime factorization is established. The remaining
# part is uniqueness of the prime factors and multiplicities up to order.
#
# Section VII — Theorem VII.2
# The power-kernel relation is developed. The actual isomorphisms identifying
# an infinite cyclic group with the integers and a finite cyclic group with
# residue classes still have to be constructed.
#
# Section VIII — Theorem VIII.1 and Corollaries 1–4
# The subgroup classification and the four stated consequences for a finite
# cyclic group have not yet been given as source-facing results.
#
# Section IX — Lemma, Theorems IX.1–IX.2, and Corollary
# Polynomial division by degree descent, principal generation of polynomial
# ideals, and the bound on the number of roots remain to be proved.
#
# Section X — Theorems X.1–X.2
# Cyclicity of finite multiplicative subgroups of a field and existence of a
# primitive root modulo a prime remain to be proved.
#
# Section XI — Theorem XI.1
# The general finite-field power-congruence criterion still needs its cyclic
# unit-group power-map proof. The quadratic specialization and the stated
# consequence classifying -1 modulo odd primes now have verifier-checked
# proof bodies, both downstream of this one remaining trusted interface.
#
# Section XII — Theorem XII.2
# Quadratic reciprocity is now checked through the two oriented lattice counts,
# their parity bridges, and the rectangle partition. Section XII has no
# executable trust.
#
# Section XIII — Lemma and Theorem XIII.1
# Rational-prime norm now implies Gaussian primality by an explicit
# equal-norm associate argument. Nearest-Gaussian-integer division is checked;
# the least-positive-norm proof that every Gaussian ideal is principal remains.
#
# Section XIII — Gaussian Euclid lemma
# Section XIII now uses native C throughout. The Gaussian carrier, addition
# and multiplication closure, and integer norm selection are checked. Native
# complex equality is transported through re/img, and the native sum/product
# coordinate formulas now support checked conjugation, norm algebra, division,
# the prime-or-Bezout route, and cancellation. Replay principal ideals and the
# remaining factorization arguments without restoring coordinate pairs.
#
# Section XIII — Theorems XIII.2–XIII.3
# Gaussian factorization traces now contain native-complex factors and partial
# products. Replay norm induction for existence and associate cancellation for
# uniqueness against this carrier.
# Rational-prime splitting remains. Theorem XIII.4 and the Gaussian-prime
# classification corollary have no direct trust; their split branches still
# inherit XIII.3's explicit upstream trust.
